      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "The number of meatless dishes is found by solving the equation $\\frac{1}{5}(x) = 4$, which gives $x = 20$. Since three of those meatless dishes contain gluten, the fraction of the menu that I can eat is $\\frac{20-3}{20} = \\boxed{\\frac{17}{20}}$.\nTo determine what fraction of the items on the menu I can eat, we start by finding the total number of dishes on the menu. According to the problem, there are four meatless dishes, which are one fifth of the entire menu. Let's denote the total number of dishes on the menu by \\( x \\). The number of meatless dishes is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{5} x = 4\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we solve the equation:\n\n\\[\nx = 4 \\times 5 = 20\n\\]\n\nSo, the total number of dishes on the menu is 20. Out of these 20 dishes, 4 are meatless and do not contain gluten. This leaves us with:\n\n\\[\n20 - 4 = 16\n\\]\n\nOf the 16 meatless dishes, 3 contain gluten. Therefore, the number of meatless dishes that do not contain gluten is:\n\n\\[\n16 - 3 = 13\n\\]\n\nThe fraction of the menu that I can eat is the number of meatless dishes that do not contain gluten divided by the total number of dishes on the menu:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{13}{20}\n\\]\n\nThus, the fraction of the items on the menu that I can eat is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{13}{20}}\n\\]",
